    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey can u help me understand this bit in an NDA i'm looking at? it's for a fintech gig. \n\n\"Notwithstanding the foregoing, nothing in this Agreement shall restrict the Receiving Party from using the Residuals (defined as ideas, concepts, know-how, or techniques retained in the unaided memory of the Receiving Party's personnel) for any purpose, provided that the Receiving Party shall not disclose any Confidential Information in violation of this Agreement.\"\n\nbasically does this mean i can still use the coding patterns i learn there for my other clients later?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, broadly speaking, this clause intends to make sure you aren't banned from using the \"residuals\" or inherent concepts you learned in your memory for later use. However, in a strict legal sense, these residuals often fail to qualify as trade secrets and may not carry the same weight, meaning you could still technically be liable if the pattern was deeply integrated and unique to your role. You should also clarify this specific definition with your legal counsel before signing, as the exact wording regarding definitions of trade secrets or general knowledge can vary significantly and impact your future employment legally.",
